{"copilot_usage":{"token_details":[{"batch_size":1000000,"cost_per_batch":500000000000,"model":"gpt-5.5","token_count":83,"token_type":"input"},{"batch_size":1000000,"cost_per_batch":50000000000,"model":"gpt-5.5","token_count":0,"token_type":"cache_read"},{"batch_size":1000000,"cost_per_batch":0,"model":"gpt-5.5","token_count":0,"token_type":"cache_write"},{"batch_size":1000000,"cost_per_batch":3000000000000,"model":"gpt-5.5","token_count":412,"token_type":"output"}],"total_nano_aiu":1277500000},"created_at":1788875400,"id":"","incomplete_details":null,"instructions":null,"max_output_tokens":2000,"model":"gpt-5.5-2026-04-23","object":"response","output":[{"content":[],"encrypted_content":"","id":"","summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"## Purpose\n\nA production HTTP gateway should enforce separate connection, first-byte, idle-stream, and total-request timeouts because each protects a different phase of request handling. The connection timeout limits how long the gateway waits to establish a TCP/TLS connection to an upstream. The first-byte timeout bounds the time until the upstream begins responding. The idle-stream timeout detects stalled response bodies after streaming has started. The total-request timeout caps the end-to-end lifetime of the request, including retries, buffering, and response transfer.\n\n## Failure modes\n\nOne timeout cannot safely replace the others because failures occur at different points and require different action. A short total timeout may abort legitimate large downloads, while a long one may leave sockets stuck during connection setup. A first-byte timeout catches overloaded or dead upstreams that accept connections but never respond, but it does not protect against a stream that sends one byte and then stalls. An idle-stream timeout handles partial streaming responses by allowing progress but terminating connections that stop making progress. Connection timeouts prevent pool exhaustion from slow handshakes or blackholed destinations.\n\nCancellation should propagate downstream and upstream: when a client disconnects or a gateway timeout fires, the gateway should cancel in-flight upstream requests, stop reading or writing bodies, and release buffers. Connection-pool cleanup is also essential: timed-out or partially consumed connections may be unsafe to reuse and should be closed unless the protocol state is known to be clean.\n\n## Operational guidance\n\nSet timeouts according to service behavior, payload size, and SLOs, and record which timeout fired for observability. Streaming APIs usually need generous total limits but strict idle limits; low-latency RPCs need tight first-byte and total limits. Coordinate timeout values across clients, gateways, and upstreams so cancellation happens predictably.\n\nOperational rule: enforce phase-specific timeouts, propagate cancellation immediately, and never rely on a single global timeout to protect all HTTP failure modes.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"","phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message"}],"output_text":null,"parallel_tool_calls":true,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt_cache_retention":"24h","reasoning":{"context":"current_turn","effort":"low","summary":null},"safety_identifier":"3e80cba422577a1d05cbd9b2fda1f8a0f9508b1d59523f38058558e44fba47f8","service_tier":"default","status":"completed","temperature":1,"text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"medium"},"tool_choice":"auto","tool_usage":{"image_gen":{"input_tokens":0,"input_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"total_tokens":0},"web_search":{"num_requests":0}},"tools":[],"top_p":0.98,"truncation":"disabled","usage":{"input_tokens":83,"input_tokens_details":{"cache_write_tokens":0,"cached_tokens":0},"output_tokens":412,"output_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":21},"total_tokens":495}}
